The ACLEDA Bank branch in Myanmar, a subsidiary of ACLEDA Bank in Cambodia, was officially launched in March last year in the form of a microfinance institution with a total capital of $ 10 million.
As of the end of 2013, MFIs were operating with a total of six branches operating in Yangon and Baguio.
Similar to ACLEDA Bank's branch, ACLEDA Bank's branch in Laos also has good financial momentum.
ACLEDA Bank Lao ABL was established in 2008 and currently has 41 branches in 16 of the 18 provinces and cities in Laos.
The bank is also equipped with 45 ATMs.
Hing Thorasi, a senior researcher at the Cambodian Institute for Cooperation and Peace (CICP), said that while the economic development situation in Cambodia, Laos and Myanmar was very similar, Cambodia could be considered a leader in the field. Finance among these three countries.
Therefore, it is a good opportunity for ACLEDA Bank to enter two countries.
According to Mr. In Channy, for 2014, ACLEDA Bank Lao plans to add four more offices, so the bank has a total of 45 branches, and also expects the loan volume to increase to $ 125 million.
Deposits will reach $ 100 million.
In Myanmar, ACLEDA Myanmar plans to set up a total of 18 branches and is expected to provide a total of $ 11 million in loans.
Debt in the eurozone remains slightly lower
Eurozone government debt levels fell for the first time in the third quarter of last year since 2007, but remain above EU limits, according to the report. According to official data released on Wednesday.
According to the Eurozone's Bureau of Statistics, the total rising debt levels in the 17-nation eurozone accounted for 92.7 percent of GDP growth. The region's debt in the third quarter of 2013 was 93.4 percent lower than in the second quarter.
EU public debt levels account for 60% of GDP growth, but most EU member states have higher debt, and the debt situation in the region has worsened as governments in the region Borrow money to fund stimulus projects or offset tax shortages during the debt crisis.
Cambodia exported 46 agricultural products, equivalent to 2.9 million tonnes, to the world market last year.
Experts in the field claim that export growth is still low compared to its potential.
According to official data from the General Department of Agriculture, the country exported more than 1 million tonnes of dried potatoes, more than 670,000 tonnes of fresh potatoes, 370,000 tonnes of rice, 200,000 tonnes of sugar, 160,000 tonnes of red corn and 80,000 tonnes of cashew nuts.
According to the report, the agricultural sector continues to play an important and far-reaching role in diversifying Cambodia's exports.
The sector currently accounts for about 27 percent of gross national product.
Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries Ouk Rabun said at the annual meeting last week that farmers have changed their habits from growing for personal use to growing for trade. .
But market access and lack of market information remain major unresolved issues for farmers.
Independent agricultural analyst Srey Chanthy argues that growth rates are still low.
We export mostly raw materials, so we lose a lot of value added to the economy.
He certainly agrees that farmers often face problems during the harvest season due to market problems and mostly rely on Vietnam and Thailand to buy at low prices.
Of course, we have a lot of open markets in the US and Europe, but the problem is that we lack the processing capacity.
But when we harvest, buyers will lower the price of our products.
Our output is still higher than Vietnam or Thailand, so we have a hard time competing with them on this price.
