Cambodian rice will have more markets abroad
Amru Rice Cambodia Exporters yesterday reached a joint venture agreement with Hanwa Corporation, a major Korean company, to set up a new company to produce and process fragrant rice for export to the Korean market. .
The deal will take effect in January through a new company, Amruen Hanwa International, and will be the first company in Cambodia to try to export rice to the Korean market.
Mr. Song Saran stated that under this agreement, Amru Rice is responsible for procuring high quality rice, processing rice that is in line with Korean standards and facilitating the export process, while Hanwa Corporation is responsible for providing capital and Find a market for rice.
In the first phase, Hanwa Corporation has prepared about $ 2 million to buy rice and will expand later.
He said that in fact, the market in Korea is a big market, but difficult to import, and in the past only Thai, American and Chinese companies can import to the Korean market.
According to Cambodian government data, there were a total of 84 rice exporters as of the end of 2013.
Amru Rice was the second largest exporter of 50,921 tonnes last year, behind Khmer Foods at 5,529 tonnes.
He added that Hanwa would achieve the new company's rice export target and contribute to the Cambodian government's goal of exporting 1 million tonnes by 2015.
Speaking at a ceremony yesterday, Cambodian Minister of Commerce Sun Chanthol praised the investment cooperation between local companies and Korea's Hanwa Corporation, and urged such cooperation to be made. Even more.
Political confrontation affects new registration growth
Political unrest and protests caused the number of new business registrations at the Ministry of Commerce to drop by more than 10 percent last year compared to the same period in 2012.
Data from the Ministry of Commerce showed that Cambodia received 2,986 new business registrations, down from 3,386, down 11.8 percent.
Ken Ratha, a spokesman for the Ministry of Commerce, said last year's election and demonstrations were one of the reasons behind the decline.
He explained other factors behind the decline, which were caused by tightening registration procedures and strengthening law enforcement.
Ratha said most of the foreign registrations came from China, Japan, Korea, Malaysia, Singapore, Vietnam and most of them focused on agriculture, especially in tourism processing, hotels and supermarkets.
Knuckey still sees the country as having great potential for trade and investment, and Cambodia remains an open and attractive place for common trade that has not changed.
