Cambodia achieves low inflation rate in 2013
Cambodia experienced a low annual inflation rate of about 4 percent in 2013.
Officials and economists said the rate reflected the improving macroeconomic and business environment, although some problems had occurred this year.
Inflation was at 4 percent in 2013, up from 3 percent in 2012, according to data released by the National Bank of Cambodia.
This rate is in line with estimates by some other international financial institutions.
Sim Lee, an official at the Institute of Statistics, said the rate of inflation was acceptable in line with past economic activity, although some imports had risen in recent months. The government is tightening tariffs.
However, we have a manageable inflation rate, so it is good for the economy.
It is because the prices of some products increase seasonally.
Gasoline, the main reason for the increase, has fluctuated slightly so it has no effect.
At the end of October last year, the government set a 60-day deadline for the director general of customs and excise to crack down on corruption in the institution.
That has pushed up the prices of some imported products.
Mr. Simly acknowledged the price impact of some imports.
The Asian Development Bank (ADB) Deputy Country Director for Cambodia, Peter Brimble, told the Post in late December that following a government directive in November to tighten legal tariffs on imports, inflationary pressures had eased. Inflation fluctuations in December 2013 rose from 2.5 percent to 3.5 percent on average and from 3.5 percent to 4.5 percent in 2014.
Telecom regulator Cambodia sets deadline for telecom companies
The Telecommunications Regulator of Cambodia (TRC) issued a notice last weekend requesting companies that have been licensed to operate telecommunications services but never proceed to report their operating updates, otherwise the regulator will revoke the license.
In a letter of notification issued on Thursday, the 16th, TRC requested that representatives of telecommunications licensees contact TRC to provide up-to-date operational information before 28 February.
Ms. Sok Chanda, CEO of Mekong Net, said that in the past, some companies had licenses to provide telecommunications services but did not operate, with some companies operating only for short periods.
Therefore, it is good to have data that is relevant to the actual operation.
Cambodia's first solar panel factory to be completed next month
A plot of land in Phnom Penh's western Por Senchey district awaiting construction is vacant and a good location for a factory.
The installation of a large factory is underway, it is designed exactly from the nearby gray workshop building.
The three-story plant, which is being built on an area of 3,200 square meters, does not use Cambodia's expensive electricity.
It can supply power on its own.
Constructed from 1,350 colorful solar panels, each panel is capable of generating 100 watts per hour of sunlight.
Estimates of energy generation in six and a half hours a day.
At about $ 50 more per unit than conventional solar panels, this solar panel will be able to depreciate for itself in three years by generating electricity and in less than 25 years of energy savings. The round will be free of cost.
It will not solve Cambodia's problem of high electricity costs, but in a country completely dependent on electricity imports from its neighbors, the solar panel option is a welcome initiative. .
Stepping into the manufacturing sector, Cambodia will be the first production hub for Star8, which plans to build more manufacturing plants in Vietnam and Malaysia.
The factory in Phnom Penh, which will be completed on February 8, will provide 150 to 250 local jobs.
Tax benefits for exports help make Cambodia an attractive location, but bringing solar technology to developing countries where new knowledge of solar panels is still part of The strategy of the company.
Construction of an additional 10,000 square meters of factory is underway at the back of an existing building.
The diversity of solar tuk-tuk technology will be among the first to be manufactured at a factory in Phnom Penh.
The parts will be made in Phnom Penh and will be exported to the world for installation, starting with Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines and Vietnam, and plans to export more to Africa.
The glass windows and solar panels fitted to the factory building will also be manufactured in Phnom Penh, which will help reduce taxes to meet customer needs.
Star8 has contracted to install solar equipment on residential buildings in Malaysia, an agreement that will include solar-powered cars for rent to students.
Star8 is working with an international food company with two test sites.
In Australia, Star8 has partnered with a major construction materials supplier to distribute its products.
Ministry of Foreign Affairs calls for facilitation of foreign visa applications
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs called on the Cambodian Consulate to speed up the visa application process and stop unofficial tariffs aimed at boosting tourism and trade, but denied that the reminders were issued due to the impact on tourism and trade. Concerns over investment.
Minister of Foreign Affairs Hor Namhong issued a directive last week, calling on the Cambodian ambassador, consul, consul general and honorary consul abroad to speed up visa procedures and charge a fixed fee. Only.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs states that the standard 30-day entry visa for Cambodia costs $ 25, including a US $ 5 processing fee.
Koy Kuong, a spokesman for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, said the circular was just a reminder to speed up visa entry into Cambodia for potential investors, including an estimated 4.9 million tourists. Visit Cambodia.
The latest official statistics from the Ministry of Foreign Affairs show that Cambodia received more than 3.7 million tourists in the first 11 months of 2013, up 18 per cent from the same period in 2012.
Of that figure, 171,561 tourists were traders, up 11.8 percent from a year earlier, while visitors from China, Japan, Korea and Taiwan, Cambodia's top foreign investors, accounted for more than 52 percent of the total. Arrival of these tourists.
Ho Vandy, co-chair of the government's private tourism working group, said the Cambodian visa application process could be speeded up, but that it was not a deciding factor for Cambodia's investment inflows.
The real problem lies in the country's political stability.
We have seen figures show an increase in tourism, but could increase even more if Cambodia does not have political instability in 2013.
Tourism contributes more than $ 2 billion a year to national income.
Exports grew strongly in 2013
Cambodia showed an increase in export volume in 2013, but it remains to be seen whether Cambodia will do better in the absence of the political stalemate caused by the July election. The next year or something?
According to a report by the Ministry of Commerce, Cambodia's export-import trade volume in 2013 reached 15.88 billion US dollars.
According to the report, Cambodia's exports of goods between January and December 2013 reached 6.90 billion US dollars, up 26 percent from the same period last year. $ 48 billion exported last year.
Mey Kalyan, a senior adviser to the Supreme National Economic Council, said Cambodia's industry deserved good results, but said it was hampered by political turmoil and then by garment industry rules.
He urged the government to keep a close eye on the widening gap between imports and exports, calling for closer scrutiny of some unnecessary imports of luxury goods.
Hiroshi Suzuki, chief economist at the Business Research Institute of Cambodia, echoed Mey Kalyan's concerns about the trade gap, but said a formal development assistance plan would be credible for foreign investment, strong tourism industry and labor costs. Competition continues to benefit Cambodia.
Although the political downturn stemmed from the July 2013 general election, he said:
The main products that Cambodia exports are mainly textiles and garments, agricultural products include rice, cassava, corn, pepper, coffee beans, soybeans, peanuts, sesame seeds, rubber, rubber, wood processing, livestock, fisheries and sugar.
The main products that Cambodia imports are mainly raw materials, garments, construction materials, vehicles and transportation of fuel, food, beverage, medicine and medical equipment, cosmetics and chemicals.
Cambodia's main trading partners include the United States, the European Union, China, Japan, South Korea, Canada, Thailand, Vietnam and Malaysia.
However, both analysts remain optimistic about the growth of the Cambodian economy, despite the volatility of the importing countries, expecting the country to work hard to boost exports. In the region more.
Thailand to focus on car exports this year
Thailand's auto industry will continue to rely on exports this year as the domestic market will be less favorable due to the country's political situation.
He predicted that 2014 car output in Thailand would be similar to last year, but this year's shipments could be 1.25 million.
According to the Thai Automobile Industry Federation, Thailand exported 1.12 million vehicles last year, up 9.88 percent from 2012, earning 512 billion baht. An increase of 4.5 percent.
In December alone, car sales fell 24.9 percent from 108,688 units in the same period last year due to a slowdown in the first car buyers' incentive program, which ended in 2012. .
However, sales rose 16.3 percent in December from November due to 41,083 car orders made during the show.
