National swimming coach dies of illness
Hem Lumphat inherited his swimming skills from his father, Hem Thon, who has been a professional swimmer for more than 10 years and has been coaching the disabled since 2011.
According to Hem Lumphat's background and experience, he has a good track record in swimming, especially from 1986 to 1998. He trained in North Korea in 1992. After graduating, he attended the 4th generation pedagogical school of physical education and sports, now the National Institute of Physical Education and Sports.
In 1995, Hem Lumphat participated in the 18th ASEAN Games in Chiang Mai, Thailand, and in 1996 he participated in the largest event, the Olympic Games in the United States.
In 2017, he attended the 19th SEA Games in Brunei and subsequently continued to work as a coach and as a member of the Federal Executive Committee.
According to Yi Veasna, secretary general of the National Paralympic Committee of Cambodia, Hem Lumphat had been suffering from abdominal pain for a week, then his family was taken to Calmette Hospital, where he was diagnosed with a stomach ulcer and was treated by doctors. His stomach was already there, but there was still blood flowing into his stomach, causing him great pain until he died.
According to the family, he also has high blood pressure.
At the same time, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, President of the Khmer Swimming Federation, who diligently helped take care of Hem Lumphat when he was admitted to the hospital, declared that he was fully responsible for the expenses for organizing Hem Lumphat's funeral. Traditionally at house No. 189 along Street 2004 in Sangkat Kakap, Khan Por Sen Chey.
According to the program, the funeral procession is scheduled for Sunday morning, and Hem Lumphat has three children in his care.
